# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

Manages automated backups for Dokploy databases. Supports scheduling backups to S3-compatible storage destinations.

## Example Usage

### Basic Database Backup

```terraform
resource "dokploy_backup" "daily" {
  database_id    = dokploy_database.postgres.id
  database_type  = "postgres"
  destination_id = dokploy_destination.s3.id
  schedule       = "0 2 * * *"  # Daily at 2 AM
  prefix         = "daily-backup"
  enabled        = true
}
```

### PostgreSQL Compose Backup

```terraform
resource "dokploy_backup" "compose_postgres" {
  backup_type       = "compose"
  compose_id        = dokploy_compose.app.id
  service_name      = "db"
  database_type     = "postgres"
  database_user     = "postgres"
  database          = "app"
  destination_id    = dokploy_destination.s3.id
  schedule          = "0 2 * * *"
  prefix            = "database"
  keep_latest_count = 30
}
```

`database_user` sends `metadata.postgres.databaseUser` to Dokploy on create and
update, and is refreshed from that nested API field. It is only supported for
`backup_type = "compose"` with `database_type = "postgres"` (the default compose
database type). Use the role configured by your PostgreSQL container.

The legacy `metadata` map remains supported for string values alongside
`database_user`; do not set its `postgres` key when using `database_user`.
Nested API fields are excluded from this string map. MySQL compose backups need
`metadata.mysql.databaseRootPassword`; MariaDB and MongoDB need
`metadata.<database_type>.databaseUser` and `databasePassword`. Those compose
credential configurations are currently unsupported by this provider.

### Existing Schedules and Rollback

Rebuild the local fork binary before using `database_user` with development
overrides. Adding the attribute updates an existing schedule in place without
changing its resource address or requiring a state migration. Review a saved
plan before applying, then run one manual backup and verify the remote dump.
Removing `database_user` clears its generated credentials on the next update;
when rolling back to an older binary, restore the user in Dokploy before
relying on scheduled compose backups.

